## Core requirements

- All controls have meaningful accessible names and keyboard operation.
- Text and controls remain usable at narrow widths and high zoom.
- Focus is visible, ordered and not hidden behind overlays.
- Meaning is available without relying on colour, shape or position alone.
- Normal text meets 4.5:1 contrast; large text and essential non-text boundaries meet their applicable 3:1 requirement.
- Errors identify the problem in text and provide a useful recovery route.
- Animation respects reduced motion; forced-colour modes retain visible controls.
- Live state updates avoid unnecessary announcements.

## Test contrast in the real state

Test each text and background pair in the state people will see. A theme colour can pass on white and fail on a tinted card. Opacity, overlays, disabled styles and colours inside images can change the result.

A decorative card border is not an input boundary. Keep them as separate tokens and test them separately.

## Test by hand as well

Automated checks cannot judge focus order, labels, screen-reader announcements, pointer targets or whether the content makes sense. Run the [manual and browser checks](testing.html) and record the result against a named release.

## Report an accessibility problem

Use the repository issue tracker. Give the page or component, the browser, the assistive technology if you used one, what you expected and what happened. Add a minimal reproduction. Do not include tokens or access credentials.

This page draws on the [GOV.UK accessibility approach](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/accessibility/) and the [Home Office Accessibility Standard](https://design.homeoffice.gov.uk/accessibility/standard), which aligns with WCAG 2.2 AA.
